 ## Overview
-Texture Coordinate Conversion maps spatial positions (typically in world or model space) into normalized texture coordinates in the $[0, 1]^3$ range required for 3D texture sampling [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of aligning geometric vertices or ray origins with the discrete texel grid of a volume texture [S1, S5, unofficial]. It differs from [Voxel Texture Atlas UV Mapping](4162-voxel-texture-atlas-uv-mapping.md) by operating on continuous 3D volumes rather than 2D atlas faces, and from [Texture Coordinate Generation for Volume](8604-texture-coordinate-generation-for-volume.md) by providing a general-purpose function for arbitrary point sampling rather than pre-computing bounding box corners [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Texture Coordinate Conversion maps spatial positions (typically in world, model, or object space) into normalized texture coordinates in the $[0, 1]^3$ range required for 3D texture sampling [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the alignment problem between continuous geometric vertices or ray origins and the discrete texel grid of a volume texture [S1, S5, unofficial]. It differs from [Voxel Texture Atlas UV Mapping](4162-voxel-texture-atlas-uv-mapping.md) by operating on continuous 3D volumes rather than 2D atlas faces [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
